# Copy to `pipeline/.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored — never commit real values.
#
# Everything except `translate`, `publish` and `seed` runs with this file empty or
# absent: scrape / images / build / verify / verify-images need no credentials at all.
# Local development needs none of it — see docs/infra.md, "Local development".

# --- translate -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Only `holo-data translate` needs this. Get a key from https://poe.com/api_key
POE_API_KEY=your_api_key_here

# --- publish -------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only `holo-data publish` needs these, and only the maintainer can publish.
# Also run: uv sync --extra publish
#
# Create the token at: Cloudflare dashboard → R2 → Manage API Tokens → Create token
#   Permission: Object Read & Write
#   Scope:      *only* the two buckets named in apps/api/wrangler.jsonc
#               (hololive-ocg-wiki-images, hololive-ocg-wiki-artifacts)
#
# Scoping to those two buckets is deliberate: the CLI may be driven by an agent (D4), and
# a token that cannot reach any other bucket bounds the damage structurally rather than
# by carefulness. See docs/infra.md for the full setup.

# Not a secret — it appears in every R2 endpoint URL. Kept here for symmetry.
# 32 hex characters, e.g. 7d0fb552073ff07340658bcefeed8a89.
# Pasting the whole S3 endpoint URL the dashboard shows
# (https://<account_id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com) also works — it is reduced to the ID.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Optional. Bucket names come from apps/api/wrangler.jsonc; these override for a one-off
# (e.g. publishing to a scratch bucket). Leave unset in normal use.
# R2_IMAGES_BUCKET=
# R2_ARTIFACTS_BUCKET=

# --- seed ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only `holo-data seed` needs these. A *different* token from the R2 one above, for the
# same reason that one is scoped to two buckets: the CLI may be driven by an agent (D4),
# and a token that structurally cannot reach another service bounds a misfire better
# than carefulness does. These cannot touch R2; the R2 keys cannot touch D1.
#
# Create at: Cloudflare dashboard → My Profile → API Tokens → Create Token → Custom
#   Account → D1 → Edit
#   Account → Account Analytics → Read
#
# Account Analytics Read is what lets `seed` check today's *actual* write usage before
# running, instead of assuming the whole 100k/day budget is free. Without it `seed` still
# works — it says it could not read usage, and only refuses if the estimate exceeds the
# full daily limit.
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# Optional. The database name and id come from apps/api/wrangler.jsonc; this overrides
# the id for a one-off (e.g. seeding a scratch database). Leave unset in normal use.
# D1_DATABASE_ID=
